---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';
import Note from '../components/Note.astro';
import { SITE_TITLE, WDS_URL } from '../consts';
---

<Base
  title="About"
  description="What Almandine's Warbox is: free tools and material for Wargame Design Studio games, starting with the WDS Scenario Picker."
>
  <PageHeader eyebrow="Site" title="About" icon="shield" lead="What this site is, and why it exists." />

  <div class="wrap section">
    <Note tone="draft">
      Draft copy. The personal parts — who Norbi is, how he got into these games, which ones he
      keeps coming back to — are his to write; this is scaffolding so the page exists and reads
      sensibly in the meantime.
    </Note>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>The short version</h2>
      <p>
        I play Wargame Design Studio games, and I build tools that make them easier to read, choose
        and keep track of. {SITE_TITLE} is where those tools live, free for anyone who plays the same
        games.
      </p>
      <p>
        The first and biggest is the <a href="/picker/">WDS Scenario Picker</a>. Alongside it are the
        Excel <a href="/series/">game companions</a> and printable material for the games.
      </p>

      <h2>Why the Scenario Picker</h2>
      <p>
        A WDS game ships with dozens of scenarios, often hundreds, and a Scenario Selector that is, in effect, a list
        of file names. Finding a short evening scenario, or the one you played last spring, means
        clicking through them one by one. The Picker puts them all in one sortable table with the full
        briefing beside it, keeps your results and notes, and starts the scenario you pick.
      </p>

      <h2>Why the companions</h2>
      <p>
        WDS games hold an enormous amount of detail, and most of it is genuinely useful once you can
        see it. The problem is never that the information is missing — it is that you are reading it
        through a small window, a few rows at a time, while trying to hold a plan in your head.
      </p>
      <p>
        So I started pulling it out into spreadsheets: one scenario per row, with room for your own
        results and notes. They were for me first. It turned out they were worth sharing.
      </p>

      <h2>What comes next</h2>
      <p>
        The Picker grows with each release, one game’s scenario data at a time. New companions and
        material appear here as they are finished. More tools for WDS games may follow; each will get
        its own page next to the Picker.
      </p>

      <h2>Not official</h2>
      <p>
        This is a fan site. It is not affiliated with Wargame Design Studio, not endorsed by them,
        and nothing here is official material. The games themselves are theirs, and they are worth
        buying — <a href={WDS_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener">wargameds.com</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Free, and staying that way</h2>
      <p>
        No account, no email, no donation button. Download what is useful to you. If you find a
        mistake or have an idea, <a href="/contact/">tell me</a> — that is payment enough.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
